    # ---------------- Chapter 13 ----------------
    ("h1", "13. ATAS: the certificate nobody mentions until it delays you"),
    ("p", "The Academic Technology Approval Scheme is a clearance certificate for certain sensitive subjects. If your course needs one, you must have it before you apply for the Student visa - not after, not in parallel. It is free, it takes time, and it is required for a lot of Indian postgraduate applicants in engineering, physical sciences, computing and biomedical fields."),
    ("h2", "Who needs it"),
    ("b", [
        "Postgraduate study or research in certain subjects - commonly master's and doctoral programmes in engineering, physics, chemistry, materials, computing, mathematics and some biomedical areas.",
        "Some integrated undergraduate master's courses that lead to a postgraduate-level qualification.",
        "Certain research visas and work routes, where the role is in a relevant subject.",
        "Nationals of some countries are exempt. India is not on the exemption list, so assume you need it if your subject qualifies.",
    ]),
    ("p", "The test is subject-based, not nationality-based. Your university will tell you whether your specific course requires ATAS, and the requirement is tied to the course's subject classification codes. Ask the admissions office in writing, and ask before you pay a deposit - a course that requires ATAS is not a problem, but discovering it two weeks before term is."),
    ("h2", "How to avoid the delay"),
    ("n", [
        "Ask admissions for the ATAS requirement and the subject codes in the same email that confirms your offer.",
        "Apply for ATAS as soon as you have the codes and your research proposal or module list, if asked for one.",
        "Do not book a visa appointment until the certificate is in hand.",
        "If your course details change - modules, supervisor, research topic - check whether the certificate needs to be reapplied for.",
        "Keep the certificate with your visa documents; some universities also require it at enrolment.",
    ]),
    ("p", "The certificate is not a visa and does not guarantee one. It clears the subject; the visa still has to clear the money, the English and the genuineness tests."),
