    # ---------------- Chapter 14 ----------------
    ("h1", "14. The TB certificate"),
    ("p", "India is on the list of countries whose residents must be screened for tuberculosis before a UK visa for more than six months. This is a formality for most people and a scheduling problem for everyone, because the certificate has a six-month life."),
    ("h2", "The rules that matter"),
    ("b", [
        "You need the test if you are coming to the UK for six months or more and you have lived in a listed country for six months or more, including within the last six months.",
        "The test must be at a clinic approved by the Home Office. A certificate from any other clinic is not accepted, however reputable.",
        "The certificate is valid for six months from the date of the x-ray.",
        "You take the passport you will use for the application, valid photo ID, the fee, and any previous chest notes if you have had TB or lung disease before.",
        "If the x-ray suggests TB, you will be referred for further tests; a previous diagnosis does not automatically prevent a visa if you are later certified free of lung TB.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "Timing it properly"),
    ("p", "Book the test so that the x-ray date falls after you know your course start date and before your application, and so that the certificate will still be valid when you travel. The ideal window is two to six weeks before you submit, which also leaves room for a repeat x-ray if the first is unclear. Approved clinics are concentrated in the bigger cities and appointments fill in the summer; in July and August, book three to four weeks ahead."),
    ("p", "Carry the certificate in hand luggage when you fly, along with your CAS and your university offer letter. Border officers do not usually ask, but the one time they do, a scanned copy on a phone with no signal is not a document."),
    ("pb", None),

